WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.098
A useful AI homelab needs basic operations habits.

00:00:03.098 --> 00:00:07.596
Monitor temperature, power, disk usage, memory, GPU utilization, service health, and logs.

00:00:07.596 --> 00:00:12.094
AI workloads can stress hardware in ways ordinary desktop use does not.

00:00:12.094 --> 00:00:15.092
High temperatures, full disks, driver crashes, runaway notebooks,

00:00:15.092 --> 00:00:18.091
and failed services can make the lab unreliable.

00:00:18.091 --> 00:00:22.589
Monitoring gives you early warning before a small problem becomes a rebuild.

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:24.837
Model inventory is part of maintenance.

00:00:24.837 --> 00:00:28.960
Keep track of which models are installed, where they came from,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.708
what license or use restrictions apply, what quantization they use,

00:00:32.708 --> 00:00:34.957
and which workflows depend on them.

00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:40.205
Without inventory, the model directory becomes a pile of large files with unclear purpose.

00:00:40.205 --> 00:00:43.953
Good inventory helps with cleanup, troubleshooting, comparison, and responsible use.

00:00:43.953 --> 00:00:45.827
Dependency updates should be deliberate.

00:00:45.827 --> 00:00:50.699
AI tooling changes quickly, and a working stack can break after a driver,

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.323
Python package, container image, or frontend update.

00:00:53.323 --> 00:00:54.822
Configuration backups, version notes,

00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:58.195
and rollback planning keep updates from becoming one-way trips.

00:00:58.195 --> 00:00:59.695
If an upgrade fails,

00:00:59.695 --> 00:01:04.942
decide whether to troubleshoot or rebuild based on the value of the current environment,

00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:08.690
the quality of documentation, and the time required to recover.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.564
Experiment notes are operational data.

00:01:10.564 --> 00:01:15.437
Record what you changed, what model you tested, what parameters mattered, what worked,

00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:16.561
and what failed.

00:01:16.561 --> 00:01:19.559
This turns random trial and error into learning.

00:01:19.559 --> 00:01:23.682
Over time, the lab should become more useful, not more fragile.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:25.931
Maintenance is not separate from experimentation.

00:01:25.931 --> 00:01:28.180
It is what keeps experimentation repeatable.
